To evaluate the computational efficiency of the compared methods, we report two key metrics during the inference stage, memory consumption and inference time, each averaged over 50 runs to reduce randomness in runtime measurements. Table 6 lists the model memory on RANPerf, which is similar across use cases for RAN-MP and NetLLM, and the inference time on all three use cases; Appendix A.x reports the model memory, inference memory, and inference time of each method on all three use cases. Auric-XGB directly uses pre-defined task-specific features with XGBoost and does not involve a separate representation-learning stage; its computational cost is therefore not directly comparable to that of methods whose pipelines include representation learning.
